{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01RYYHjWXxiyvPmM2s8Z54fx","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t## CONTEXT\n2\t\n3\tPrepare an English technical report for project `AR-S2J2J.R`. The project uses measured blast vibration records scaled to scenario amplitudes. The objective is to construct scenario records for TSF dynamic response review.\n4\t\n5\t## SLOTS\n6\t\n7\t### SLOT 1: Support for amplitude-scaling measured records\n8\t\n9\tAssess what support exists in blasting, geotechnical earthquake engineering, or transient dynamic analysis literature for amplitude-scaling measured records for scenario studies. Write in flowing paragraphs.\n10\t\n11\t### SLOT 2: Preserved and non-preserved properties\n12\t\n13\tExplain which properties simple amplitude scaling preserves and which properties it does not preserve, including frequency content, duration, phase relationships, component coherency, wave propagation effects, source/path/site terms, cycle count, and pore-pressure-relevant cumulative demand. Write in flowing paragraphs.\n14\t\n15\t### SLOT 3: Independent component-peak scaling assumptions and limitations\n16\t\n17\tState the assumptions and limitations that should be declared for TSF dynamic response assessment when measured records are scaled independently by component peaks. Write in flowing paragraphs.\n18\t\n19\t### SLOT 4: Distinction from other waveform-adjustment methods\n20\t\n21\tExplain how amplitude scaling differs from spectral matching, target-spectrum adjustment, synthetic waveform generation, and calibrated blast source modelling. Write in flowing paragraphs.\n22\t\n23\t### SLOT 5: Defensible wording for scenario demand histories\n24\t\n25\tDevelop defensible wording for describing scaled measured blast records as scenario demand histories for saturated-tailings response. Write in flowing paragraphs.\n26\t\n27\t## CONSTRAINTS\n28\t\n29\t- The output is for an English technical report.\n30\t- The work must construct scenario records for TSF dynamic response review, not perform spectral matching.\n31\t- The work must not synthesize a physics-based blast source.\n32\t- The work must not claim direct pore-pressure prediction from acceleration records alone.\n33\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"28219691-6b67-4fbf-b214-045292e7efcf","uuid":"8820ab01-59ea-48ff-893f-41ae04de0549","timestamp":"2026-07-06T20:51:35.379Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/averrik/Cloud/github/projects/AR-S2J2J.R/TITO/md/gap-tsf-blast-record-scaling-limitations.prompt.md","content":"## CONTEXT\n\nPrepare an English technical report for project `AR-S2J2J.R`. The project uses measured blast vibration records scaled to scenario amplitudes. The objective is to construct scenario records for TSF dynamic response review.\n\n## SLOTS\n\n### SLOT 1: Support for amplitude-scaling measured records\n\nAssess what support exists in blasting, geotechnical earthquake engineering, or transient dynamic analysis literature for amplitude-scaling measured records for scenario studies. Write in flowing paragraphs.\n\n### SLOT 2: Preserved and non-preserved properties\n\nExplain which properties simple amplitude scaling preserves and which properties it does not preserve, including frequency content, duration, phase relationships, component coherency, wave propagation effects, source/path/site terms, cycle count, and pore-pressure-relevant cumulative demand. Write in flowing paragraphs.\n\n### SLOT 3: Independent component-peak scaling assumptions and limitations\n\nState the assumptions and limitations that should be declared for TSF dynamic response assessment when measured records are scaled independently by component peaks. Write in flowing paragraphs.\n\n### SLOT 4: Distinction from other waveform-adjustment methods\n\nExplain how amplitude scaling differs from spectral matching, target-spectrum adjustment, synthetic waveform generation, and calibrated blast source modelling. Write in flowing paragraphs.\n\n### SLOT 5: Defensible wording for scenario demand histories\n\nDevelop defensible wording for describing scaled measured blast records as scenario demand histories for saturated-tailings response. Write in flowing paragraphs.\n\n## CONSTRAINTS\n\n- The output is for an English technical report.\n- The work must construct scenario records for TSF dynamic response review, not perform spectral matching.\n- The work must not synthesize a physics-based blast source.\n- The work must not claim direct pore-pressure prediction from acceleration records alone.\n","numLines":33,"startLine":1,"totalLines":33}}}
