{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01F7Sb6hUzMfkvuB7ASyJmae","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"amplitude scaling ground motion records seismic dynamic analysis limitations geotechnical\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Does amplitude scaling of ground motion records result in biased nonlinear structural drift responses?\",\"url\":\"https://pubs.usgs.gov/publication/70031683\"},{\"title\":\"(PDF) Scaling of Earthquake Ground Motion Records for Seismic Analysis and Design of Bridges\",\"url\":\"https://www.academia.edu/22843157/Scaling_of_Earthquake_Ground_Motion_Records_for_Seismic_Analysis_and_Design_of_Bridges\"},{\"title\":\"Does amplitude scaling of ground motion records result in biased nonlinear structural drift responses? - Luco - 2007 - Earthquake Engineering & Structural Dynamics - Wiley Online Library\",\"url\":\"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1002/eqe.695\"},{\"title\":\"Evaluation the effect of amplitude scaling of real ground motions on seismic demands accounting different structural characteristics and soil classes | Bulletin of Earthquake Engineering | Springer Nature Link\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10518-023-01780-1\"},{\"title\":\"(PDF) Evaluation of Ground-Motion Record Scaling and Selection Methods for Seismic Design and Assessment of Structures\",\"url\":\"https://www.academia.edu/101279705/Evaluation_of_Ground_Motion_Record_Scaling_and_Selection_Methods_for_Seismic_Design_and_Assessment_of_Structures\"},{\"title\":\"Amplitude-Scaling Bias Analysis of Ground Motion Record Set in Strip Method for Structural Seismic Fragility Assessment\",\"url\":\"https://www.mdpi.com/2075-5309/15/3/401\"},{\"title\":\"Amplitude scaling of ground motions as a potential source of bias: Large‐scale investigations on structural drifts - Tsalouchidis - 2022 - Earthquake Engineering & Structural Dynamics - Wiley Online Library\",\"url\":\"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/full/10.1002/eqe.3707\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, here's what I found about amplitude scaling of ground motion records for seismic dynamic analysis:\n\n## Overview of Amplitude Scaling\n\nAmplitude scaling is the process of modifying ground motion records with moderate or lower intensity to achieve a higher intensity by multiplying or dividing the acceleration of a ground motion record by a scaling factor to match a targeted value of amplitude or spectrum.\n\n## Why Amplitude Scaling is Used\n\nLimitations of the existing earthquake ground motion database lead to scaling of records to obtain seismograms consistent with a ground motion target for structural design and evaluation. Amplitude scaling and selection of ground-motion records are frequently used tools to obtain a group of accelerograms suitable for structural performance assessment for a given hazard level.\n\n## Scaling Limitations and Concerns\n\nIn the engineering seismology community, acceptable limits for 'legitimate' scaling vary from one (no scaling allowed) to 10 or more, with concerns mostly based on differences in ground motion characteristics for different earthquake magnitude-distance scenarios. The adequate level of spectral match between ground-motion records and the target is a critical issue as it could lead to biased results.\n\n## Spectrum-Compatible Scaling Benefits\n\nIntensity-based scaling methods are preferred in performance-based design because they preserve the original non-stationary content of records while modifying only their amplitude, which leads to more accurate estimations of engineering demand parameters during seismic analysis.\n\n## Recent Research Findings\n\nRecent research indicates that scaled records can be safely used in nonlinear response history analysis to assess seismic structural behavior if spectral and scenario compatibility are ensured and the sustained amplitude is also consistent.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"28219691-6b67-4fbf-b214-045292e7efcf","uuid":"feff68ba-8db9-4e46-be4a-34f158d6df6b","timestamp":"2026-07-06T20:52:10.344Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"amplitude scaling ground motion records seismic dynamic analysis limitations geotechnical","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_017s8Hz1NTJyjePeFLksgXUC","content":[{"title":"Does amplitude scaling of ground motion records result in biased nonlinear structural drift responses?","url":"https://pubs.usgs.gov/publication/70031683"},{"title":"(PDF) Scaling of Earthquake Ground Motion Records for Seismic Analysis and Design of Bridges","url":"https://www.academia.edu/22843157/Scaling_of_Earthquake_Ground_Motion_Records_for_Seismic_Analysis_and_Design_of_Bridges"},{"title":"Does amplitude scaling of ground motion records result in biased nonlinear structural drift responses? - Luco - 2007 - Earthquake Engineering & Structural Dynamics - Wiley Online Library","url":"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1002/eqe.695"},{"title":"Evaluation the effect of amplitude scaling of real ground motions on seismic demands accounting different structural characteristics and soil classes | Bulletin of Earthquake Engineering | Springer Nature Link","url":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10518-023-01780-1"},{"title":"(PDF) Evaluation of Ground-Motion Record Scaling and Selection Methods for Seismic Design and Assessment of Structures","url":"https://www.academia.edu/101279705/Evaluation_of_Ground_Motion_Record_Scaling_and_Selection_Methods_for_Seismic_Design_and_Assessment_of_Structures"},{"title":"Amplitude-Scaling Bias Analysis of Ground Motion Record Set in Strip Method for Structural Seismic Fragility Assessment","url":"https://www.mdpi.com/2075-5309/15/3/401"},{"title":"Amplitude scaling of ground motions as a potential source of bias: Large‐scale investigations on structural drifts - Tsalouchidis - 2022 - Earthquake Engineering & Structural Dynamics - Wiley Online Library","url":"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/full/10.1002/eqe.3707"}]},"Based on the search results, here's what I found about amplitude scaling of ground motion records for seismic dynamic analysis:\n\n## Overview of Amplitude Scaling\n\nAmplitude scaling is the process of modifying ground motion records with moderate or lower intensity to achieve a higher intensity by multiplying or dividing the acceleration of a ground motion record by a scaling factor to match a targeted value of amplitude or spectrum.\n\n## Why Amplitude Scaling is Used\n\nLimitations of the existing earthquake ground motion database lead to scaling of records to obtain seismograms consistent with a ground motion target for structural design and evaluation. Amplitude scaling and selection of ground-motion records are frequently used tools to obtain a group of accelerograms suitable for structural performance assessment for a given hazard level.\n\n## Scaling Limitations and Concerns\n\nIn the engineering seismology community, acceptable limits for 'legitimate' scaling vary from one (no scaling allowed) to 10 or more, with concerns mostly based on differences in ground motion characteristics for different earthquake magnitude-distance scenarios. The adequate level of spectral match between ground-motion records and the target is a critical issue as it could lead to biased results.\n\n## Spectrum-Compatible Scaling Benefits\n\nIntensity-based scaling methods are preferred in performance-based design because they preserve the original non-stationary content of records while modifying only their amplitude, which leads to more accurate estimations of engineering demand parameters during seismic analysis.\n\n## Recent Research Findings\n\nRecent research indicates that scaled records can be safely used in nonlinear response history analysis to assess seismic structural behavior if spectral and scenario compatibility are ensured and the sustained amplitude is also consistent."],"durationSeconds":7.316170457999993,"searchCount":1}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01PSDnw1tYjP59X7AgZYBETd","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"blast vibration record amplitude scaling scenario geotechnical dynamic response review\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Dynamic characteristics and damage recognition of blast-induced ground vibration for natural gas transmission pipeline and its integrated systems - ScienceDirect\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0888327019306934\"},{\"title\":\"(PDF) Modified scaled distance regression analysis approach for prediction of blast-induced ground vibration in multi-hole blasting\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/328607748_Modified_scaled_distance_regression_analysis_approach_for_prediction_of_blast-induced_ground_vibration_in_multi-hole_blasting\"},{\"title\":\"(PDF) Blast-induced ground vibrations: a dynamic analysis by FEM\",\"url\":\"https://www.academia.edu/129437489/Blast_induced_ground_vibrations_a_dynamic_analysis_by_FEM\"},{\"title\":\"Frontiers | Study on blast-induced ground vibration velocity limits for slope rock masses\",\"url\":\"https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/earth-science/articles/10.3389/feart.2022.1098630/full\"},{\"title\":\"Blast-induced ground vibrations: a dynamic analysis by FEM | Research, Society and Development\",\"url\":\"https://rsdjournal.org/rsd/article/view/35421\"},{\"title\":\"Bulletin 656 BLASTING VIBRATIONS AND .THEIR EFFECTS ON STRUCTURES\",\"url\":\"https://www.resolutionmineeis.us/sites/default/files/references/nicholls-johnson-duvall-1971.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Improved probabilistic seismic demand-intensity relationship:   heteroskedastic approachs\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2201.06698\"},{\"title\":\"Research Review on Blast Vibration Intensity, Waveform and Spectrum: Prediction and Active Control\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciopen.com/article/10.3963/j.issn.1001-487X.2024.03.023\"}]\n\nI've performed the web search for your query on blast vibration record amplitude scaling in geotechnical dynamic response scenarios. Here are the key findings:\n\n## Blast-Induced Ground Vibration Analysis\n\n**Peak Particle Velocity (PPV) as a Key Parameter**\n\nPeak particle velocity (PPV) is generally a significant parameter of causing destructive failure, and in blasting engineering, the blasting disturbance is mainly evaluated by the peak vibration velocity of ground particles.\n\n**Factors Affecting Blast Vibration**\n\nThe blast-induced ground vibration level at a distance depends on vibration frequency, amplitude, duration, ground characteristics (type, unit weight, etc.), and explosive conditions.\n\n**Amplitude Scaling in Dynamic Analysis**\n\nIt is necessary to scale the selected ground motion records to achieve the desired levels of seismic intensity. Amplitude scaling of the seismic record to the target earthquake intensity may cause a change in the shape of the earthquake spectrum, resulting in a difference between the structural responses generated by the amplitude-scaling ground motion record and the real ground motion record at that seismic intensity.\n\n**Prediction Methods**\n\nNumerical analysis of seismic vibrations has been demonstrated to be a method that can significantly contribute to predicting PPV, with the finite element method (FEM) used to assess blasting-induced ground vibration in rock masses.\n\nThe search results provide comprehensive coverage of blast vibration analysis, amplitude scaling considerations, and geotechnical dynamic response evaluation methods.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"28219691-6b67-4fbf-b214-045292e7efcf","uuid":"fca91446-24fe-4454-ac4b-2acb5de6aecd","timestamp":"2026-07-06T20:52:17.027Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"blast vibration record amplitude scaling scenario geotechnical dynamic response review","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_018TxCrhrmbCsR1fkZd5pJYv","content":[{"title":"Dynamic characteristics and damage recognition of blast-induced ground vibration for natural gas transmission pipeline and its integrated systems - ScienceDirect","url":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0888327019306934"},{"title":"(PDF) Modified scaled distance regression analysis approach for prediction of blast-induced ground vibration in multi-hole blasting","url":"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/328607748_Modified_scaled_distance_regression_analysis_approach_for_prediction_of_blast-induced_ground_vibration_in_multi-hole_blasting"},{"title":"(PDF) Blast-induced ground vibrations: a dynamic analysis by FEM","url":"https://www.academia.edu/129437489/Blast_induced_ground_vibrations_a_dynamic_analysis_by_FEM"},{"title":"Frontiers | Study on blast-induced ground vibration velocity limits for slope rock masses","url":"https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/earth-science/articles/10.3389/feart.2022.1098630/full"},{"title":"Blast-induced ground vibrations: a dynamic analysis by FEM | Research, Society and Development","url":"https://rsdjournal.org/rsd/article/view/35421"},{"title":"Bulletin 656 BLASTING VIBRATIONS AND .THEIR EFFECTS ON STRUCTURES","url":"https://www.resolutionmineeis.us/sites/default/files/references/nicholls-johnson-duvall-1971.pdf"},{"title":"Improved probabilistic seismic demand-intensity relationship:   heteroskedastic approachs","url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2201.06698"},{"title":"Research Review on Blast Vibration Intensity, Waveform and Spectrum: Prediction and Active Control","url":"https://www.sciopen.com/article/10.3963/j.issn.1001-487X.2024.03.023"}]},"I've performed the web search for your query on blast vibration record amplitude scaling in geotechnical dynamic response scenarios. Here are the key findings:\n\n## Blast-Induced Ground Vibration Analysis\n\n**Peak Particle Velocity (PPV) as a Key Parameter**\n\nPeak particle velocity (PPV) is generally a significant parameter of causing destructive failure, and in blasting engineering, the blasting disturbance is mainly evaluated by the peak vibration velocity of ground particles.\n\n**Factors Affecting Blast Vibration**\n\nThe blast-induced ground vibration level at a distance depends on vibration frequency, amplitude, duration, ground characteristics (type, unit weight, etc.), and explosive conditions.\n\n**Amplitude Scaling in Dynamic Analysis**\n\nIt is necessary to scale the selected ground motion records to achieve the desired levels of seismic intensity. Amplitude scaling of the seismic record to the target earthquake intensity may cause a change in the shape of the earthquake spectrum, resulting in a difference between the structural responses generated by the amplitude-scaling ground motion record and the real ground motion record at that seismic intensity.\n\n**Prediction Methods**\n\nNumerical analysis of seismic vibrations has been demonstrated to be a method that can significantly contribute to predicting PPV, with the finite element method (FEM) used to assess blasting-induced ground vibration in rock masses.\n\nThe search results provide comprehensive coverage of blast vibration analysis, amplitude scaling considerations, and geotechnical dynamic response evaluation methods."],"durationSeconds":13.498631834,"searchCount":1}}
