{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01K8MughZEkxkt39FB4KP8kQ","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"Luco Bazzurro 2007 amplitude scaling ground motion records nonlinear drift bias\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Does amplitude scaling of ground motion records result in biased nonlinear drift responses? | Request PDF\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/227933785_Does_amplitude_scaling_of_ground_motion_records_result_in_biased_nonlinear_drift_responses\"},{\"title\":\"Does amplitude scaling of ground motion records result in biased nonlinear structural drift responses? - Luco - 2007 - Earthquake Engineering & Structural Dynamics - Wiley Online Library\",\"url\":\"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1002/eqe.695\"},{\"title\":\"Amplitude-Scaling Bias Analysis of Ground Motion Record Set in Strip Method for Structural Seismic Fragility Assessment\",\"url\":\"https://www.mdpi.com/2075-5309/15/3/401\"},{\"title\":\"Amplitude scaling of ground motions as a potential source of bias: Large‐scale investigations on structural drifts - Tsalouchidis - 2022 - Earthquake Engineering & Structural Dynamics - Wiley Online Library\",\"url\":\"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/abs/10.1002/eqe.3707\"},{\"title\":\"Does amplitude scaling of ground motion records result in biased nonlinear structural drift responses? | Semantic Scholar\",\"url\":\"https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/Does-amplitude-scaling-of-ground-motion-records-in-Luco-Bazzurro/a0db241deb213e8ed3e8195005da5ba5c425d4a7\"},{\"title\":\"Scaling bias and record selection for fragility analysis\",\"url\":\"https://iitk.ac.in/nicee/wcee/article/WCEE2012_4338.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"(PDF) Amplitude-Scaling Bias Analysis of Ground Motion Record Set in Strip Method for Structural Seismic Fragility Assessment\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/388428328_Amplitude-Scaling_Bias_Analysis_of_Ground_Motion_Record_Set_in_Strip_Method_for_Structural_Seismic_Fragility_Assessment\"},{\"title\":\"SELECTING AND SCALING OF REAL ACCELEROGRAMS TO REDUCE THE\",\"url\":\"https://users.metu.edu.tr/ozer/documents/publications/C5.pdf\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, I found relevant information about the Luco and Bazzurro 2007 study on amplitude scaling of ground motion records and nonlinear drift bias.\n\n## Overview\n\nThe study investigates whether scaling of records randomly selected from an Mw–Rclose bin to a target fundamental-mode spectral acceleration (Sa) level introduces bias in the expected nonlinear structural drift response of both single-degree-of-freedom oscillators and one multi-degree-of-freedom building.\n\n## Key Findings\n\nLuco and Bazzurro studied the nonlinear structural drift response before and after scaling records randomly selected from an M-R bin, and concluded that scaling does introduce bias, and the bias is associated with the scaling factor and the M-R range of scaling records.\n\nMore specifically, scaling the amplitude of a record to the target pseudo-spectral acceleration level at the fundamental period of structure introduces bias that can be reduced by selecting records having similar shapes to the target spectrum.\n\n## Context\n\nLimitations of the existing earthquake ground motion database lead to scaling of records to obtain seismograms consistent with a ground motion target for structural design and evaluation, and in the engineering seismology community, acceptable limits for 'legitimate' scaling vary from one (no scaling allowed) to 10 or more.\n\nThe paper was published in the journal *Earthquake Engineering & Structural Dynamics* in 2007 (Volume 36, pages 1813-1835).\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"28219691-6b67-4fbf-b214-045292e7efcf","uuid":"cb797890-1844-427d-a0ec-a47f28d9ace1","timestamp":"2026-07-06T20:52:28.316Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"Luco Bazzurro 2007 amplitude scaling ground motion records nonlinear drift bias","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_012PBxM3tQ42utHjpaxxGLNm","content":[{"title":"Does amplitude scaling of ground motion records result in biased nonlinear drift responses? | Request PDF","url":"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/227933785_Does_amplitude_scaling_of_ground_motion_records_result_in_biased_nonlinear_drift_responses"},{"title":"Does amplitude scaling of ground motion records result in biased nonlinear structural drift responses? - Luco - 2007 - Earthquake Engineering & Structural Dynamics - Wiley Online Library","url":"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1002/eqe.695"},{"title":"Amplitude-Scaling Bias Analysis of Ground Motion Record Set in Strip Method for Structural Seismic Fragility Assessment","url":"https://www.mdpi.com/2075-5309/15/3/401"},{"title":"Amplitude scaling of ground motions as a potential source of bias: Large‐scale investigations on structural drifts - Tsalouchidis - 2022 - Earthquake Engineering & Structural Dynamics - Wiley Online Library","url":"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/abs/10.1002/eqe.3707"},{"title":"Does amplitude scaling of ground motion records result in biased nonlinear structural drift responses? | Semantic Scholar","url":"https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/Does-amplitude-scaling-of-ground-motion-records-in-Luco-Bazzurro/a0db241deb213e8ed3e8195005da5ba5c425d4a7"},{"title":"Scaling bias and record selection for fragility analysis","url":"https://iitk.ac.in/nicee/wcee/article/WCEE2012_4338.pdf"},{"title":"(PDF) Amplitude-Scaling Bias Analysis of Ground Motion Record Set in Strip Method for Structural Seismic Fragility Assessment","url":"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/388428328_Amplitude-Scaling_Bias_Analysis_of_Ground_Motion_Record_Set_in_Strip_Method_for_Structural_Seismic_Fragility_Assessment"},{"title":"SELECTING AND SCALING OF REAL ACCELEROGRAMS TO REDUCE THE","url":"https://users.metu.edu.tr/ozer/documents/publications/C5.pdf"}]},"Based on the search results, I found relevant information about the Luco and Bazzurro 2007 study on amplitude scaling of ground motion records and nonlinear drift bias.\n\n## Overview\n\nThe study investigates whether scaling of records randomly selected from an Mw–Rclose bin to a target fundamental-mode spectral acceleration (Sa) level introduces bias in the expected nonlinear structural drift response of both single-degree-of-freedom oscillators and one multi-degree-of-freedom building.\n\n## Key Findings\n\nLuco and Bazzurro studied the nonlinear structural drift response before and after scaling records randomly selected from an M-R bin, and concluded that scaling does introduce bias, and the bias is associated with the scaling factor and the M-R range of scaling records.\n\nMore specifically, scaling the amplitude of a record to the target pseudo-spectral acceleration level at the fundamental period of structure introduces bias that can be reduced by selecting records having similar shapes to the target spectrum.\n\n## Context\n\nLimitations of the existing earthquake ground motion database lead to scaling of records to obtain seismograms consistent with a ground motion target for structural design and evaluation, and in the engineering seismology community, acceptable limits for 'legitimate' scaling vary from one (no scaling allowed) to 10 or more.\n\nThe paper was published in the journal *Earthquake Engineering & Structural Dynamics* in 2007 (Volume 36, pages 1813-1835)."],"durationSeconds":6.876641582999997,"searchCount":1}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01KarNRUZaQDFdutNmHzfVzf","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"record scaling preserves frequency content phase duration cycle count limitations seismic\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Benchmark strong ground motion records: statistical analysis of response variability and selection bias | Journal of Umm Al-Qura University for Engineering and Architecture | Springer Nature Link\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s43995-025-00220-2\"},{\"title\":\"Frontiers | Selecting and Scaling of Energy-Compatible Ground Motion Records\",\"url\":\"https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/built-environment/articles/10.3389/fbuil.2019.00140/full\"},{\"title\":\"(PDF) Selection and Scaling Time History Records for Performance-Based Design\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/313800752_Selection_and_Scaling_Time_History_Records_for_Performance-Based_Design\"},{\"title\":\"Multidimensional amplitude scaling of seismic data\",\"url\":\"https://image-ppubs.uspto.gov/dirsearch-public/print/downloadPdf/4312050\"},{\"title\":\"Moment‐Duration Scaling of Low‐Frequency Earthquakes in Guerrero, Mexico - Farge - 2020 - Journal of Geophysical Research: Solid Earth - Wiley Online Library\",\"url\":\"https://agupubs.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/full/10.1029/2019JB019099\"},{\"title\":\"Seismological Understanding of Accelerogram Amplitude Scaling for   Engineers with Implications to Seismic Risk Analysis\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2305.05631\"},{\"title\":\"Moment-duration scaling of Low-Frequency Earthquakes in ...\",\"url\":\"https://dspace.mit.edu/bitstream/handle/1721.1/133633/Farge_etal_GRL2020.pdf?sequence=2&isAllowed=y\"},{\"title\":\"Broadband Ground Motion Synthesis via Generative Adversarial Neural   Operators: Development and Validation\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2309.03447\"},{\"title\":\"Incorporation of Strong Motion Duration in Incremental-based Seismic   Assessments\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1910.08076\"}]\n\nBased on the web search results, here are the key findings related to seismic record scaling and its effects on frequency content, phase, duration, and cycle count:\n\n## Record Scaling and Its Preservation Properties\n\nAccelerogram amplitude scaling entails multiplying a recorded accelerogram by a scale factor so as to intensify the amplitudes without changing neither the duration nor the frequency content of the recording. Both recorded (i.e., unscaled) and scaled accelerograms have the same start and end times with zero phase shift between their amplitudes.\n\n## Limitations and Concerns\n\nHowever, there are important limitations to this idealized view. Duration scaling may not preserve the damage potential of near-fault ground motions. Additionally, adjusting the Fourier spectrum of a real ground motion in the frequency domain with a view to matching a target spectrum at specific frequencies affects amplitude, frequency content and phasing, which generally tends to increase the total input energy.\n\nGenerated accelerograms often have an excessive number of cycles of strong motion, and consequently have unrealistically high energy content.\n\n## Practical Implications\n\nThe spectral shapes as well as the duration of the selected records are kept constant during the process of record scaling. However, scaling or modification of records that is not based on physics may only scale some intensities or frequency measures while leaving other elements unaltered, leading to unrealistic ground motions.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"28219691-6b67-4fbf-b214-045292e7efcf","uuid":"eff66552-8422-4289-b68f-e258afac6b98","timestamp":"2026-07-06T20:52:28.906Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"record scaling preserves frequency content phase duration cycle count limitations seismic","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_01SvbDU5LmHTwcFnxatAYNdA","content":[{"title":"Benchmark strong ground motion records: statistical analysis of response variability and selection bias | Journal of Umm Al-Qura University for Engineering and Architecture | Springer Nature Link","url":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s43995-025-00220-2"},{"title":"Frontiers | Selecting and Scaling of Energy-Compatible Ground Motion Records","url":"https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/built-environment/articles/10.3389/fbuil.2019.00140/full"},{"title":"(PDF) Selection and Scaling Time History Records for Performance-Based Design","url":"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/313800752_Selection_and_Scaling_Time_History_Records_for_Performance-Based_Design"},{"title":"Multidimensional amplitude scaling of seismic data","url":"https://image-ppubs.uspto.gov/dirsearch-public/print/downloadPdf/4312050"},{"title":"Moment‐Duration Scaling of Low‐Frequency Earthquakes in Guerrero, Mexico - Farge - 2020 - Journal of Geophysical Research: Solid Earth - Wiley Online Library","url":"https://agupubs.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/full/10.1029/2019JB019099"},{"title":"Seismological Understanding of Accelerogram Amplitude Scaling for   Engineers with Implications to Seismic Risk Analysis","url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2305.05631"},{"title":"Moment-duration scaling of Low-Frequency Earthquakes in ...","url":"https://dspace.mit.edu/bitstream/handle/1721.1/133633/Farge_etal_GRL2020.pdf?sequence=2&isAllowed=y"},{"title":"Broadband Ground Motion Synthesis via Generative Adversarial Neural   Operators: Development and Validation","url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2309.03447"},{"title":"Incorporation of Strong Motion Duration in Incremental-based Seismic   Assessments","url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1910.08076"}]},"Based on the web search results, here are the key findings related to seismic record scaling and its effects on frequency content, phase, duration, and cycle count:\n\n## Record Scaling and Its Preservation Properties\n\nAccelerogram amplitude scaling entails multiplying a recorded accelerogram by a scale factor so as to intensify the amplitudes without changing neither the duration nor the frequency content of the recording. Both recorded (i.e., unscaled) and scaled accelerograms have the same start and end times with zero phase shift between their amplitudes.\n\n## Limitations and Concerns\n\nHowever, there are important limitations to this idealized view. Duration scaling may not preserve the damage potential of near-fault ground motions. Additionally, adjusting the Fourier spectrum of a real ground motion in the frequency domain with a view to matching a target spectrum at specific frequencies affects amplitude, frequency content and phasing, which generally tends to increase the total input energy.\n\nGenerated accelerograms often have an excessive number of cycles of strong motion, and consequently have unrealistically high energy content.\n\n## Practical Implications\n\nThe spectral shapes as well as the duration of the selected records are kept constant during the process of record scaling. However, scaling or modification of records that is not based on physics may only scale some intensities or frequency measures while leaving other elements unaltered, leading to unrealistic ground motions."],"durationSeconds":6.077205499999996,"searchCount":1}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01MGz3GB94aWQykcQmsEUUjo","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"spectral matching vs amplitude scaling ground motion records differences geotechnical embankment dam\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Appropriate Earthquake Loads for Design of Embankment Dams - ATC Williams\",\"url\":\"https://atcwilliams.com/appropriate-earthquake-loads-for-design-of-embankment-dams/\"},{\"title\":\"Comparison of Ground Motion Estimation Methods on Embankment Dam Deformation | Association of State Dam Safety\",\"url\":\"https://damsafety.org/reference/comparison-ground-motion-estimation-methods-embankment-dam-deformation\"},{\"title\":\"Selection and spectral matching of recorded ground motions for seismic fragility analyses | Bulletin of Earthquake Engineering | Springer Nature Link\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10518-022-01393-0\"},{\"title\":\"Seismic Deformation Analysis: A Practical Guide to Dam Resilience - GFT\",\"url\":\"https://www.gftinc.com/blog/seismic-deformation-analysis-a-practical-guide-to-dam-resilience/\"},{\"title\":\"Embankment vibration characteristics using ground motion records and ambient noise measurements, Briones Dam, California - ScienceDirect\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0038080625000988\"},{\"title\":\"An Improved Approach for Ground Motion Suite Selection ...\",\"url\":\"https://www.iitk.ac.in/nicee/wcee/article/WCEE2012_5752.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Seismic response analysis of embankment dams under decomposed earthquakes | Request PDF\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/340478778_Seismic_response_analysis_of_embankment_dams_under_decomposed_earthquakes\"},{\"title\":\"Nitinol Belleville elastic nonlinear (Ni-Belle-E-N) structural assembly\",\"url\":\"https://image-ppubs.uspto.gov/dirsearch-public/print/downloadPdf/12180742\"},{\"title\":\"Introducing a response-based duration metric and its correlation with   structural damages\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1906.10604\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, here are the key differences between spectral matching and amplitude scaling for ground motion records in geotechnical embankment dam applications:\n\n## Definition and Approach\n\nSeed records are adjusted by either scaling them linearly or by manipulating the frequency content using spectral matching techniques. Linear scaling uses constant factor scaling in the time domain, while spectral matching techniques adjust records to approach the target response spectrum either by adding wavelet components to the original time series or by iteratively scaling the Fourier spectrum amplitude of the original record.\n\n## Key Advantages of Amplitude Scaling\n\nAmplitude scaling has advantages resulting from the only modification being amplitude scaling, as records maintain all individual characteristics of the original record except amplitude, with the characteristic period and energy content of the record maintained, as well as the variability between periods and records.\n\n## Key Limitations of Spectral Matching\n\nThe main advantage of spectral scaling is that the epistemic variation of the original earthquake motion is considered, though the scaled earthquake only matches the site response spectrum at one or a limited number of frequencies.\n\n## Application in Dam Analysis\n\nFor embankment dams specifically, spectral matching is an efficient technique for calculating the expected performance, and linear scaling can provide an appreciation for the expected variability. Studies compare embankment deformations using design ground motions generated by these two different approaches, such as comparing maximum deformation results from spectrally matched ground motions to average results from linearly scaled ground motions.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"28219691-6b67-4fbf-b214-045292e7efcf","uuid":"8454fa9a-5a93-425c-8480-019f631c3c92","timestamp":"2026-07-06T20:52:29.514Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"spectral matching vs amplitude scaling ground motion records differences geotechnical embankment dam","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_01LBCNhDcDtrykww5kxRVVDE","content":[{"title":"Appropriate Earthquake Loads for Design of Embankment Dams - ATC Williams","url":"https://atcwilliams.com/appropriate-earthquake-loads-for-design-of-embankment-dams/"},{"title":"Comparison of Ground Motion Estimation Methods on Embankment Dam Deformation | Association of State Dam Safety","url":"https://damsafety.org/reference/comparison-ground-motion-estimation-methods-embankment-dam-deformation"},{"title":"Selection and spectral matching of recorded ground motions for seismic fragility analyses | Bulletin of Earthquake Engineering | Springer Nature Link","url":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10518-022-01393-0"},{"title":"Seismic Deformation Analysis: A Practical Guide to Dam Resilience - GFT","url":"https://www.gftinc.com/blog/seismic-deformation-analysis-a-practical-guide-to-dam-resilience/"},{"title":"Embankment vibration characteristics using ground motion records and ambient noise measurements, Briones Dam, California - ScienceDirect","url":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0038080625000988"},{"title":"An Improved Approach for Ground Motion Suite Selection ...","url":"https://www.iitk.ac.in/nicee/wcee/article/WCEE2012_5752.pdf"},{"title":"Seismic response analysis of embankment dams under decomposed earthquakes | Request PDF","url":"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/340478778_Seismic_response_analysis_of_embankment_dams_under_decomposed_earthquakes"},{"title":"Nitinol Belleville elastic nonlinear (Ni-Belle-E-N) structural assembly","url":"https://image-ppubs.uspto.gov/dirsearch-public/print/downloadPdf/12180742"},{"title":"Introducing a response-based duration metric and its correlation with   structural damages","url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1906.10604"}]},"Based on the search results, here are the key differences between spectral matching and amplitude scaling for ground motion records in geotechnical embankment dam applications:\n\n## Definition and Approach\n\nSeed records are adjusted by either scaling them linearly or by manipulating the frequency content using spectral matching techniques. Linear scaling uses constant factor scaling in the time domain, while spectral matching techniques adjust records to approach the target response spectrum either by adding wavelet components to the original time series or by iteratively scaling the Fourier spectrum amplitude of the original record.\n\n## Key Advantages of Amplitude Scaling\n\nAmplitude scaling has advantages resulting from the only modification being amplitude scaling, as records maintain all individual characteristics of the original record except amplitude, with the characteristic period and energy content of the record maintained, as well as the variability between periods and records.\n\n## Key Limitations of Spectral Matching\n\nThe main advantage of spectral scaling is that the epistemic variation of the original earthquake motion is considered, though the scaled earthquake only matches the site response spectrum at one or a limited number of frequencies.\n\n## Application in Dam Analysis\n\nFor embankment dams specifically, spectral matching is an efficient technique for calculating the expected performance, and linear scaling can provide an appreciation for the expected variability. Studies compare embankment deformations using design ground motions generated by these two different approaches, such as comparing maximum deformation results from spectrally matched ground motions to average results from linearly scaled ground motions."],"durationSeconds":7.404078666000001,"searchCount":1}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01ELHBCV4HWja1LxuXk2L4Lm","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"tailings dam seismic analysis blast vibration pore pressure cyclic loading demand\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"(PDF) Analysis and Seismic Design of Tailings Dams and Liquefaction Assessment\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/337801086_Analysis_and_Seismic_Design_of_Tailings_Dams_and_Liquefaction_Assessment\"},{\"title\":\"Seismic stability of coal tailings dams with spatially variable and liquefiable coal tailings using pore pressure plasticity models - ScienceDirect\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0266352X21000203\"},{\"title\":\"Frontiers | Experimental Study of Dynamic Characteristics of Tailings With Different Reconsolidation Degrees After Liquefaction\",\"url\":\"https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/earth-science/articles/10.3389/feart.2022.876401/full\"},{\"title\":\"Seismic Performance of a Tailings Dam Located in Hight Seismicity Area | Bellido | ASEAN Journal of Science and Engineering\",\"url\":\"https://ejournal.upi.edu/index.php/AJSE/article/view/75557\"},{\"title\":\"Dynamic characterization of tailing dam using fully coupled dynamic analysis with different boundary conditions — a case study | Earthquake Engineering and Engineering Vibration | Springer Nature Link\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11803-023-2218-4\"},{\"title\":\"Mitigating Risks for Tailings Dams with Microseismic Technology - Tetra Tech\",\"url\":\"https://www.tetratech.com/insights/mitigating-risks-for-tailings-dams-with-microseismic-technology/\"},{\"title\":\"Field Investigation and Stability Analysis of Coal Slurry ...\",\"url\":\"https://www.osmre.gov/sites/default/files/asp-files/S16AC20074-FinalReport.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"(PDF) Seismic assesment of a tailings dam designed by the downstream method located in a zone of high seismicity\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/368883128_Seismic_assesment_of_a_tailings_dam_designed_by_the_downstream_method_located_in_a_zone_of_high_seismicity\"}]\n\nI've successfully completed the web search for information about tailings dam seismic analysis, blast vibration, pore pressure, and cyclic loading demand. Here's a summary of the key findings:\n\n## Key Research Findings\n\n**Cyclic Loading Effects:**\nUnder cyclic loading, the accumulation of pore water pressure was faster in the fine tailings than that in the coarse tailings. Cyclic loadings such as earthquakes and blasting are among the main threats to the stability of tailings dams.\n\n**Seismic Analysis Methods:**\nNonlinear deformation analyses (NDAs) were conducted using advanced constitutive models: PM4Silt (designed to simulate the behavior of clay and plastic silts under cyclic loading, with an emphasis on excess pore pressure generation) and UBCHyst (developed to model soils that do not generate excess pore pressure during cyclic loading).\n\n**Blast Vibration and Pore Pressure:**\nThe peak particle velocity (PPV) is often used to determine the blast loading imposed on infrastructures and to evaluate the excess pore water pressure ratio.\n\n**Liquefaction Susceptibility:**\nThe reconsolidation degree significantly affects the trend of the excess pore water pressure ratio changing with the increase in the cycle number of loads.\n\nThe search results include peer-reviewed research articles and technical studies focused on the geotechnical engineering aspects of tailings dam stability under dynamic loadings.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"28219691-6b67-4fbf-b214-045292e7efcf","uuid":"9cdfcb06-e062-4c1a-b7cd-093f6191dad9","timestamp":"2026-07-06T20:52:30.263Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"tailings dam seismic analysis blast vibration pore pressure cyclic loading demand","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_011WCZJpVS9ALDguXwogw2xo","content":[{"title":"(PDF) Analysis and Seismic Design of Tailings Dams and Liquefaction Assessment","url":"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/337801086_Analysis_and_Seismic_Design_of_Tailings_Dams_and_Liquefaction_Assessment"},{"title":"Seismic stability of coal tailings dams with spatially variable and liquefiable coal tailings using pore pressure plasticity models - ScienceDirect","url":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0266352X21000203"},{"title":"Frontiers | Experimental Study of Dynamic Characteristics of Tailings With Different Reconsolidation Degrees After Liquefaction","url":"https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/earth-science/articles/10.3389/feart.2022.876401/full"},{"title":"Seismic Performance of a Tailings Dam Located in Hight Seismicity Area | Bellido | ASEAN Journal of Science and Engineering","url":"https://ejournal.upi.edu/index.php/AJSE/article/view/75557"},{"title":"Dynamic characterization of tailing dam using fully coupled dynamic analysis with different boundary conditions — a case study | Earthquake Engineering and Engineering Vibration | Springer Nature Link","url":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11803-023-2218-4"},{"title":"Mitigating Risks for Tailings Dams with Microseismic Technology - Tetra Tech","url":"https://www.tetratech.com/insights/mitigating-risks-for-tailings-dams-with-microseismic-technology/"},{"title":"Field Investigation and Stability Analysis of Coal Slurry ...","url":"https://www.osmre.gov/sites/default/files/asp-files/S16AC20074-FinalReport.pdf"},{"title":"(PDF) Seismic assesment of a tailings dam designed by the downstream method located in a zone of high seismicity","url":"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/368883128_Seismic_assesment_of_a_tailings_dam_designed_by_the_downstream_method_located_in_a_zone_of_high_seismicity"}]},"I've successfully completed the web search for information about tailings dam seismic analysis, blast vibration, pore pressure, and cyclic loading demand. Here's a summary of the key findings:\n\n## Key Research Findings\n\n**Cyclic Loading Effects:**\nUnder cyclic loading, the accumulation of pore water pressure was faster in the fine tailings than that in the coarse tailings. Cyclic loadings such as earthquakes and blasting are among the main threats to the stability of tailings dams.\n\n**Seismic Analysis Methods:**\nNonlinear deformation analyses (NDAs) were conducted using advanced constitutive models: PM4Silt (designed to simulate the behavior of clay and plastic silts under cyclic loading, with an emphasis on excess pore pressure generation) and UBCHyst (developed to model soils that do not generate excess pore pressure during cyclic loading).\n\n**Blast Vibration and Pore Pressure:**\nThe peak particle velocity (PPV) is often used to determine the blast loading imposed on infrastructures and to evaluate the excess pore water pressure ratio.\n\n**Liquefaction Susceptibility:**\nThe reconsolidation degree significantly affects the trend of the excess pore water pressure ratio changing with the increase in the cycle number of loads.\n\nThe search results include peer-reviewed research articles and technical studies focused on the geotechnical engineering aspects of tailings dam stability under dynamic loadings."],"durationSeconds":7.1276975839999945,"searchCount":1}}
