{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01XVAsfKfx1E5B4wZ38yS3UE","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"Bommer Acevedo 2004 \"real accelerograms\" selection scaling ground motion seismic design\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Structure-specific selection of earthquake ground motions for the reliable design and assessment of structures | Bulletin of Earthquake Engineering\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10518-017-0226-3\"},{\"title\":\"SELECTING AND SCALING OF REAL ACCELEROGRAMS TO REDUCE THE\",\"url\":\"https://users.metu.edu.tr/ozer/documents/publications/C5.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Engineering ground motion record selection in the ITalian ACcelerometric Archive | Bulletin of Earthquake Engineering | Springer Nature Link\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10518-011-9300-4\"},{\"title\":\"Selection of real earthquake accelerograms for structural dynamic analysis and energy evaluation | Request PDF\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/251417733_Selection_of_real_earthquake_accelerograms_for_structural_dynamic_analysis_and_energy_evaluation\"},{\"title\":\"Selection and Scaling Approaches of Earthquake Time-Series for Structural Engineering Applications: A State-of-the-Art Review | Archives of Computational Methods in Engineering | Springer Nature Link\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11831-023-10025-y\"},{\"title\":\"A Review of Ground Motion Record Selection Strategies for Dynamic Structural Analysis | Springer Nature Link\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-3-211-09445-7_3\"},{\"title\":\"Site-specific seismic design of damage tolerant structural systems using a novel concept | Bulletin of Earthquake Engineering | Springer Nature Link\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10518-018-0329-5\"},{\"title\":\"(PDF) The use of real accelerograms as input to dynamic analysis\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/233460611_The_use_of_real_accelerograms_as_input_to_dynamic_analysis\"},{\"title\":\"THE USE OF REAL EARTHQUAKE ACCELEROGRAMS AS INPUT TO DYNAMIC ANALYSIS | Semantic Scholar\",\"url\":\"https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/THE-USE-OF-REAL-EARTHQUAKE-ACCELEROGRAMS-AS-INPUT-Bommer-Acevedo/84f53edfeb3a19a7e9601645ac5bfc09795ec228\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, I found substantial information about the Bommer and Acevedo 2004 paper on real accelerograms for seismic design.\n\n## Summary of Bommer & Acevedo (2004)\n\nThe paper is titled \"The use of real earthquake accelerograms as input to dynamic analysis\" and was published in the Journal of Earthquake Engineering, Volume 8, pages 43-91.\n\n### Key Contributions\n\nThe paper addresses the increasing availability of strong-motion accelerograms and their use as input to dynamic analyses in geotechnical and structural engineering, while noting that guidelines on procedures for selection of appropriate suites of acceleration time-series are lacking and seismic design codes are particularly poor in this respect.\n\n### Selection Criteria\n\nBommer and Acevedo investigated several record selection criteria and proposed a simple one based on magnitude, distance and site class. The authors studied the influence of geophysical and response spectral search criteria and highlighted the importance of achieving a good match with the design earthquake magnitude and the site classification of the project.\n\n### Scaling and Adjustment\n\nThe paper presents criteria for selecting records in terms of earthquake scenarios and in terms of response spectral ordinates, together with options and criteria for adjusting the selected accelerograms to match the elastic design spectrum.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"28219691-6b67-4fbf-b214-045292e7efcf","uuid":"62337ae2-a678-43cd-b181-0d4a3402d597","timestamp":"2026-07-06T20:52:41.988Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"Bommer Acevedo 2004 \"real accelerograms\" selection scaling ground motion seismic design","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_012CUYkUULQd4nS4jfCXveuh","content":[{"title":"Structure-specific selection of earthquake ground motions for the reliable design and assessment of structures | Bulletin of Earthquake Engineering","url":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10518-017-0226-3"},{"title":"SELECTING AND SCALING OF REAL ACCELEROGRAMS TO REDUCE THE","url":"https://users.metu.edu.tr/ozer/documents/publications/C5.pdf"},{"title":"Engineering ground motion record selection in the ITalian ACcelerometric Archive | Bulletin of Earthquake Engineering | Springer Nature Link","url":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10518-011-9300-4"},{"title":"Selection of real earthquake accelerograms for structural dynamic analysis and energy evaluation | Request PDF","url":"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/251417733_Selection_of_real_earthquake_accelerograms_for_structural_dynamic_analysis_and_energy_evaluation"},{"title":"Selection and Scaling Approaches of Earthquake Time-Series for Structural Engineering Applications: A State-of-the-Art Review | Archives of Computational Methods in Engineering | Springer Nature Link","url":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11831-023-10025-y"},{"title":"A Review of Ground Motion Record Selection Strategies for Dynamic Structural Analysis | Springer Nature Link","url":"https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-3-211-09445-7_3"},{"title":"Site-specific seismic design of damage tolerant structural systems using a novel concept | Bulletin of Earthquake Engineering | Springer Nature Link","url":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10518-018-0329-5"},{"title":"(PDF) The use of real accelerograms as input to dynamic analysis","url":"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/233460611_The_use_of_real_accelerograms_as_input_to_dynamic_analysis"},{"title":"THE USE OF REAL EARTHQUAKE ACCELEROGRAMS AS INPUT TO DYNAMIC ANALYSIS | Semantic Scholar","url":"https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/THE-USE-OF-REAL-EARTHQUAKE-ACCELEROGRAMS-AS-INPUT-Bommer-Acevedo/84f53edfeb3a19a7e9601645ac5bfc09795ec228"}]},"Based on the search results, I found substantial information about the Bommer and Acevedo 2004 paper on real accelerograms for seismic design.\n\n## Summary of Bommer & Acevedo (2004)\n\nThe paper is titled \"The use of real earthquake accelerograms as input to dynamic analysis\" and was published in the Journal of Earthquake Engineering, Volume 8, pages 43-91.\n\n### Key Contributions\n\nThe paper addresses the increasing availability of strong-motion accelerograms and their use as input to dynamic analyses in geotechnical and structural engineering, while noting that guidelines on procedures for selection of appropriate suites of acceleration time-series are lacking and seismic design codes are particularly poor in this respect.\n\n### Selection Criteria\n\nBommer and Acevedo investigated several record selection criteria and proposed a simple one based on magnitude, distance and site class. The authors studied the influence of geophysical and response spectral search criteria and highlighted the importance of achieving a good match with the design earthquake magnitude and the site classification of the project.\n\n### Scaling and Adjustment\n\nThe paper presents criteria for selecting records in terms of earthquake scenarios and in terms of response spectral ordinates, together with options and criteria for adjusting the selected accelerograms to match the elastic design spectrum."],"durationSeconds":5.6735212499999905,"searchCount":1}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_013NEXcxPZfSF7Dvh1QJ1Wwm","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"PEER NGA ground motion selection scaling guidelines frequency content duration preserving\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"an automated open‑source ground motion record selection ...\",\"url\":\"https://gerardjoreilly.github.io/files/Journal/J24-2021.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Benchmark strong ground motion records: statistical analysis of response variability and selection bias | Journal of Umm Al-Qura University for Engineering and Architecture | Springer Nature Link\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s43995-025-00220-2\"},{\"title\":\"2.4.2. PEER NGA Records — Earthquake Engineering with Uncertainty Quantification documentation\",\"url\":\"https://nheri-simcenter.github.io/EE-UQ-Documentation/common/user_manual/usage/desktop/earthquake/peerNGA.html\"},{\"title\":\"NIST GCR 11-917-15 Selecting and Scaling Earthquake Ground\",\"url\":\"https://www.nehrp.gov/pdf/nistgcr11-917-15.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Introducing a response-based duration metric and its correlation with   structural damages\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1906.10604\"},{\"title\":\"PEER Ground Motion Database – Beta Version Technical Report i\",\"url\":\"https://ngawest2.berkeley.edu/Technical_Report.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"PEER Ground Motion Database - Users Manual for the\",\"url\":\"https://ngawest2.berkeley.edu/peer_ground_motion_database_users_manual.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"The selection and scaling of ground motion records for ...\",\"url\":\"https://www.iitk.ac.in/nicee/wcee/article/WCEE2012_0186.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Ground motion selection and scaling for seismic design and assessment of structures in Canada: a critical review\",\"url\":\"https://cdnsciencepub.com/doi/10.1139/cjce-2025-0153\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, here's what I found regarding PEER NGA ground motion selection and scaling with consideration for frequency content and duration:\n\n## Ground Motion Selection and Scaling Overview\n\nThe PGMD selects acceleration records from the PEER-NGA database that satisfy user-specified selection criteria and provide good fits to the target response spectrum. Three different scaling methods are available: (1) no scaling, (2) scaling factors applied individually to records to minimize differences between mean spectra (Minimize MSE), and (3) scaling factors applied individually to pin all spectra at a given period (Single Period).\n\n## Frequency Content Considerations\n\nBecause of the suppression of ground motion at frequencies lower than the lowest usable frequency, it is recommended that selected records have lowest usable frequencies equal to or lower than the lowest frequency of interest. Additionally, the demand computed for a chosen parameter will depend on the frequency content of the ground motion input, and the best method for selecting and scaling ground motions, and the range of period used for scaling to a target spectrum, will therefore vary as a function of the analyst's choice of parameters.\n\n## Duration Preservation\n\nReal accelerograms have a realistic duration given the earthquake scenario, frequency content and correlation between the vertical and horizontal components of ground motion and also between the phase and the amplitude of the record. Search criteria may include significant duration range (D_S5-95) and limits on the scale factor.\n\n## Spectral Matching Approach\n\nRecords are scaled by minimizing the average difference between their spectra and a target spectrum over a frequency range, preserving natural frequencies.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"28219691-6b67-4fbf-b214-045292e7efcf","uuid":"adab646b-1574-41f3-8624-62caa3685fff","timestamp":"2026-07-06T20:52:43.841Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"PEER NGA ground motion selection scaling guidelines frequency content duration preserving","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_01FDMqQzMpj2bAUEwFyvfHG4","content":[{"title":"an automated open‑source ground motion record selection ...","url":"https://gerardjoreilly.github.io/files/Journal/J24-2021.pdf"},{"title":"Benchmark strong ground motion records: statistical analysis of response variability and selection bias | Journal of Umm Al-Qura University for Engineering and Architecture | Springer Nature Link","url":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s43995-025-00220-2"},{"title":"2.4.2. PEER NGA Records — Earthquake Engineering with Uncertainty Quantification documentation","url":"https://nheri-simcenter.github.io/EE-UQ-Documentation/common/user_manual/usage/desktop/earthquake/peerNGA.html"},{"title":"NIST GCR 11-917-15 Selecting and Scaling Earthquake Ground","url":"https://www.nehrp.gov/pdf/nistgcr11-917-15.pdf"},{"title":"Introducing a response-based duration metric and its correlation with   structural damages","url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1906.10604"},{"title":"PEER Ground Motion Database – Beta Version Technical Report i","url":"https://ngawest2.berkeley.edu/Technical_Report.pdf"},{"title":"PEER Ground Motion Database - Users Manual for the","url":"https://ngawest2.berkeley.edu/peer_ground_motion_database_users_manual.pdf"},{"title":"The selection and scaling of ground motion records for ...","url":"https://www.iitk.ac.in/nicee/wcee/article/WCEE2012_0186.pdf"},{"title":"Ground motion selection and scaling for seismic design and assessment of structures in Canada: a critical review","url":"https://cdnsciencepub.com/doi/10.1139/cjce-2025-0153"}]},"Based on the search results, here's what I found regarding PEER NGA ground motion selection and scaling with consideration for frequency content and duration:\n\n## Ground Motion Selection and Scaling Overview\n\nThe PGMD selects acceleration records from the PEER-NGA database that satisfy user-specified selection criteria and provide good fits to the target response spectrum. Three different scaling methods are available: (1) no scaling, (2) scaling factors applied individually to records to minimize differences between mean spectra (Minimize MSE), and (3) scaling factors applied individually to pin all spectra at a given period (Single Period).\n\n## Frequency Content Considerations\n\nBecause of the suppression of ground motion at frequencies lower than the lowest usable frequency, it is recommended that selected records have lowest usable frequencies equal to or lower than the lowest frequency of interest. Additionally, the demand computed for a chosen parameter will depend on the frequency content of the ground motion input, and the best method for selecting and scaling ground motions, and the range of period used for scaling to a target spectrum, will therefore vary as a function of the analyst's choice of parameters.\n\n## Duration Preservation\n\nReal accelerograms have a realistic duration given the earthquake scenario, frequency content and correlation between the vertical and horizontal components of ground motion and also between the phase and the amplitude of the record. Search criteria may include significant duration range (D_S5-95) and limits on the scale factor.\n\n## Spectral Matching Approach\n\nRecords are scaled by minimizing the average difference between their spectra and a target spectrum over a frequency range, preserving natural frequencies."],"durationSeconds":7.152113916000002,"searchCount":1}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01CDvD5e3Wc4jnvzfrgjCRpS","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"synthetic waveform generation blast source model calibrated stochastic vs measured records comparison\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Nobel Fire | Dyno Nobel\",\"url\":\"https://www.dynonobel.com/products-services/products/software/nobel-fire/\"},{\"title\":\"High Resolution Seismic Waveform Generation using Denoising Diffusion\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2410.19343v1\"},{\"title\":\"Recorded Versus Synthetic Spectral-compatible Ground Motions: A Comparative Analysis of Structural Seismic Responses\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2502.19549\"},{\"title\":\"High Resolution Seismic Waveform Generation using Denoising Diffusion\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2410.19343\"},{\"title\":\"Noise attenuation via thresholding in a transform domain\",\"url\":\"https://image-ppubs.uspto.gov/dirsearch-public/print/downloadPdf/10317554\"},{\"title\":\"Data-Driven Forecasting of three-Component Seismograms Using Transformer Architectures\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2606.02912\"},{\"title\":\"Waveform cross correlation for seismic monitoring of underground nuclear   explosions. Part II: Synthetic master events\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1305.3245\"},{\"title\":\"Method for customizing seismic explosives\",\"url\":\"https://image-ppubs.uspto.gov/dirsearch-public/print/downloadPdf/6490529\"},{\"title\":\"A computer vision-based approach to clean seismic catalogues\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2509.00791\"}]\n\nI've found several relevant research results for your search on synthetic waveform generation, blast source modeling, and comparisons between stochastic and measured records:\n\n## Key Findings:\n\n**Stochastic Waveform Generation:**\nAdvanced Vibration Prediction uses a proprietary waveform generation algorithm that starts with a seed waveform and generates thousands of synthetic waveforms to add variability to synthetic blasting outcomes. These iterations provide a stochastic analysis that gives the likelihood of all potential outcomes for vibration prediction.\n\n**Model Calibration with Measured Data:**\nThrough an automated calibration process, fragmentation models can be calibrated using a single or series of measured blasts, making the model extremely accurate for operation-specific geology.\n\n**Synthetic vs. Measured Records Comparison:**\nResearch presents comparative analyses of synthetic motions generated by stochastic spectral-compatible ground motion models versus recorded motions, with both datasets calibrated to a shared target response spectrum to ensure consistent spectral median, variance, and correlation structure. Results reveal that synthetic motions closely replicate recorded motions in terms of global response behavior including distributions, mean and variance, and correlation structure.\n\n**Waveform Synthesis Methods:**\nFrameworks for synthetics generation require modeling of synthetic waveforms for seismic events given source locations, focal mechanisms and a velocity model, plus stochastic simulation of noise waveforms.\n\nThese results cover the key aspects of your query regarding synthetic waveform generation, blast source models, and calibrated comparisons between synthetic and measured records.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"28219691-6b67-4fbf-b214-045292e7efcf","uuid":"742e25ba-f35b-4417-98f5-255f040e843b","timestamp":"2026-07-06T20:52:44.513Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"synthetic waveform generation blast source model calibrated stochastic vs measured records comparison","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_01WmpgWDq4mdTXgG4GAor5HD","content":[{"title":"Nobel Fire | Dyno Nobel","url":"https://www.dynonobel.com/products-services/products/software/nobel-fire/"},{"title":"High Resolution Seismic Waveform Generation using Denoising Diffusion","url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2410.19343v1"},{"title":"Recorded Versus Synthetic Spectral-compatible Ground Motions: A Comparative Analysis of Structural Seismic Responses","url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2502.19549"},{"title":"High Resolution Seismic Waveform Generation using Denoising Diffusion","url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2410.19343"},{"title":"Noise attenuation via thresholding in a transform domain","url":"https://image-ppubs.uspto.gov/dirsearch-public/print/downloadPdf/10317554"},{"title":"Data-Driven Forecasting of three-Component Seismograms Using Transformer Architectures","url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2606.02912"},{"title":"Waveform cross correlation for seismic monitoring of underground nuclear   explosions. Part II: Synthetic master events","url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1305.3245"},{"title":"Method for customizing seismic explosives","url":"https://image-ppubs.uspto.gov/dirsearch-public/print/downloadPdf/6490529"},{"title":"A computer vision-based approach to clean seismic catalogues","url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2509.00791"}]},"I've found several relevant research results for your search on synthetic waveform generation, blast source modeling, and comparisons between stochastic and measured records:\n\n## Key Findings:\n\n**Stochastic Waveform Generation:**\nAdvanced Vibration Prediction uses a proprietary waveform generation algorithm that starts with a seed waveform and generates thousands of synthetic waveforms to add variability to synthetic blasting outcomes. These iterations provide a stochastic analysis that gives the likelihood of all potential outcomes for vibration prediction.\n\n**Model Calibration with Measured Data:**\nThrough an automated calibration process, fragmentation models can be calibrated using a single or series of measured blasts, making the model extremely accurate for operation-specific geology.\n\n**Synthetic vs. Measured Records Comparison:**\nResearch presents comparative analyses of synthetic motions generated by stochastic spectral-compatible ground motion models versus recorded motions, with both datasets calibrated to a shared target response spectrum to ensure consistent spectral median, variance, and correlation structure. Results reveal that synthetic motions closely replicate recorded motions in terms of global response behavior including distributions, mean and variance, and correlation structure.\n\n**Waveform Synthesis Methods:**\nFrameworks for synthetics generation require modeling of synthetic waveforms for seismic events given source locations, focal mechanisms and a velocity model, plus stochastic simulation of noise waveforms.\n\nThese results cover the key aspects of your query regarding synthetic waveform generation, blast source models, and calibrated comparisons between synthetic and measured records."],"durationSeconds":7.086806499999992,"searchCount":1}}
