paragraph to introduce and interpret the formula separately with their own footnotes, or simplify by removing the formula entirely and just discussing the concept. The second approach seems cleaner—I can mention Arias intensity without embedding the actual equation. I'm considering whether to display the Arias intensity formula separately or just describe it in prose to keep the explanation cleaner. The key insight is that after scaling by $SF$, the intensity at every time point scales by $SF^2$, which means the percentile times defining $D_{5-95}$ stay the same—so I can convey this without the integral notation cluttering the text.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":87977,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":87977,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":21,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"28219691-6b67-4fbf-b214-045292e7efcf","uuid":"9c11d760-6dda-4e5f-a1f5-a78ba0ed9a6f","request_id":"req_011CcmUbs4kmBtC4iV1dH5Ry"}
